{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["עֵקֶב אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַע אַבְרָהָם בְּקֹלִי — regarding the Akedah, as it is written: אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַעְתָּ בְּקֹלִי." "וְיִשְׁמֹר מִשְׁמַרְתִּי — such as circumcision, about which it is written: וְאַתָּה אֶת־בְּרִיתִי תִּשְׁמֹר." "מִצְוֺתַי — such as the commandment of eight days, as it is written: כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה אֹתוֹ אֱלֹהִים." "חֻקּוֹתַי וְתוֹרֹתַי — according to its essential plain sense: the recognizable commandments (= the rational ones), such as theft and forbidden sexual relations, and coveting, and civil laws, and hosting guests — all of these were practiced before the giving of the Torah, except that they were renewed and spelled out explicitly for Israel, and they entered a covenant to observe them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {3 "The parenthetical (מצות) appears in the Hebrew source; the gloss השכליות ('the rational ones') is a variant reading for הניכרות ('the recognizable ones'), here both rendered as Rashbam appears to present them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:45:20.574614082Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}